    "description": "A media-import service runs three worker threads that block on a shared in-memory work queue. Each item is a batch that must be fully written to a staging directory and recorded in the database before the worker asks for another item. During deployment, the service receives a termination request while the producer may still be enqueueing files. The system must stop accepting new work, ensure every item already in the queue is processed exactly once, and then let all three workers leave their blocking wait. Killing the process is not acceptable because it can leave half-written batches and incomplete database records. A separate control connection to each worker would complicate deployment and require every worker to implement a second waiting mechanism. The queue has no native close operation, so the shutdown design must use only ordinary item messages and must deliver one unambiguous end-of-stream indication to each worker.",
    "decision_statement": "How should the service tell all blocked workers to finish the queued batches and exit without interrupting work already in progress?",
    "notes": "poison-pill fits three queue-blocked workers that must drain the backlog and exit via ordinary item messages; structured-concurrency is the near miss for scope-owned shutdown, with cancellation-token supporting the stop-accepting signal."
  },
